# Cursor Rule Authoring Standard

> Every new `.mdc` rule file must follow this standard. When creating or editing a rule, read this file first.

---

## Rule File Structure

Every `.mdc` file must have exactly this structure, in this order:

```
---
description: <one concise sentence — what this rule governs>
globs: <file patterns this rule activates on>
alwaysApply: <true | false>
---

# <Rule Title>

## <Section>
...
```

### Frontmatter Fields

| Field | Required | Notes |
|-------|----------|-------|
| `description` | ✅ | One sentence. No period at end. Used by Cursor to decide when to load the rule |
| `globs` | ✅ | Comma-separated glob patterns. Use `**/*` only for truly universal rules |
| `alwaysApply` | ✅ | `true` only for cross-cutting rules (security, DRY/KISS/YAGNI, git). Default `false` |

### `alwaysApply` Decision

```
Does this rule apply regardless of file type or feature?
├── YES (e.g. security, code principles, git) → alwaysApply: true
└── NO (e.g. C rules, React rules, SQL rules) → alwaysApply: false
    └── Set precise globs so the rule only loads when relevant
```

---

## Naming Conventions

- File names: `kebab-case.mdc`
- Be specific: `nextjs-shadcn-components.mdc` not `frontend.mdc`
- Group by concern, not by tool: `rest-api-design.mdc` not `express-routes.mdc`
- Names must be self-explanatory without reading the file

### Existing Rules in This Project

| File | Scope | alwaysApply |
|------|-------|-------------|
| `self-improve.mdc` | AI behaviour | true |
| `dry-kiss-yagni.mdc` | All code | true |
| `oop-best-practices.mdc` | `.java .cpp .cs .py .hpp` | false |
| `c-best-practices.mdc` | `.c .h` | false |
| `react-shadcn-typescript.mdc` | `.tsx .ts` | false |
| `nextjs-shadcn-components.mdc` | `.tsx .ts app/ components/` | false |
| `rule-authoring.mdc` | `.mdc` | false |

**Update this table every time a new rule is added.**

