## Cooperation Between Rules — The Most Important Section

Rules must be aware of each other. Before writing a new rule:

### Step 1 — Check for Overlap
Read every existing rule that shares globs with your new rule. Ask:
- Does an existing rule already cover this topic?
- Would my new rule contradict an existing rule?
- Could I add a section to an existing rule instead of creating a new file?

### Step 2 — Resolve Conflicts Explicitly
If two rules touch the same topic, one must **defer to the other**. Use this pattern at the top of the relevant section:

```md
> This section works alongside `oop-best-practices.mdc`.
> For class design rules, see that file. This file covers React-specific patterns only.
```

### Step 3 — Cross-Reference, Don't Duplicate
If another rule covers something relevant, **link it** rather than restating it:

```md
> Error handling patterns follow `node-express-nestjs.mdc § Error Handling`.
> This rule does not redefine them.
```

### Step 4 — Layer Rules Correctly

Rules form a hierarchy. Lower layers defer to higher ones:

```
Level 1 — Universal (alwaysApply: true)
  self-improve.mdc
  dry-kiss-yagni.mdc

Level 2 — Language (activates on file extension)
  oop-best-practices.mdc     → .java .cs .cpp .py
  c-best-practices.mdc       → .c .h

Level 3 — Framework (activates on extension + folder pattern)
  react-shadcn-typescript.mdc   → .tsx .ts
  nextjs-shadcn-components.mdc  → .tsx .ts + app/ components/

Level 4 — Workflow (activates on specific file types or always)
  rule-authoring.mdc         → .mdc
```

**A Level 3 rule never redefines what Level 1 already mandates.**
If DRY/KISS/YAGNI covers it, your framework rule must not repeat it — reference it instead.

