## Checklist — Before Saving a New Rule

### Structure
- [ ] Frontmatter has `description`, `globs`, and `alwaysApply`
- [ ] `description` is one sentence and accurately describes the scope
- [ ] `globs` are as specific as possible — not `**/*` unless truly universal
- [ ] `alwaysApply: true` is justified (cross-cutting concern)
- [ ] File is named in `kebab-case.mdc`

### Cooperation
- [ ] I read every existing rule that shares my globs
- [ ] My rule does not contradict any existing rule
- [ ] My rule does not duplicate content already in another rule
- [ ] Where overlap exists, I reference the other rule explicitly
- [ ] The table in `rule-authoring.mdc § Existing Rules` is updated

### Content
- [ ] Each section has a clear, measurable directive
- [ ] Non-obvious rules have ✅ / ❌ code examples
- [ ] No section restates a Level 1 rule (DRY/KISS/YAGNI, self-improve) — it references it
- [ ] The rule fits its layer (Universal → Language → Framework → Workflow)

### Maintenance
- [ ] Rule is scoped tightly enough that it won't need editing for unrelated reasons
- [ ] If this rule obsoletes part of another rule, that other rule is updated

---

## When to Create vs. Extend vs. Delete

### Create a new rule when
- A new language, framework, or tool is added to the project
- A recurring review comment has no rule backing it
- A new workflow concern (testing strategy, deployment, API design) is established

### Extend an existing rule when
- The new content belongs to the same concern and same glob pattern
- The existing file is under the target length
- The new section cooperates naturally with existing sections

### Delete or merge a rule when
- A framework is removed from the project
- Two rules have significant overlap and one can absorb the other cleanly
- A rule has not been referenced or triggered in a long time (YAGNI)

---

## Anti-Patterns

| ❌ Anti-pattern | ✅ Correct approach |
|----------------|---------------------|
| `alwaysApply: true` on a framework-specific rule | Set precise globs; `alwaysApply: false` |
| Restating DRY/KISS/YAGNI rules in a framework rule | Add `> See dry-kiss-yagni.mdc` reference |
| Two rules with contradicting naming conventions | One rule wins; the other defers explicitly |
| A rule file covering 5 unrelated concerns | Split into focused, single-concern files |
| Glob `**/*` on a language-specific rule | Use `**/*.ts, **/*.tsx` etc. |
| No code examples in a non-obvious rule | Add ✅ / ❌ examples for every key directive |
| Creating a rule for a one-off case | Inline the guidance as a comment instead |
| Forgetting to update the Existing Rules table | Always update it — it is the rule registry |
